course: dictionary
lesson: 1910
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word comb is a good example. «She ran a comb through her wet hair.» Here a comb is a tool you use to tidy your hair.
explain: «The police combed the woods for the missing child.» Police do not tidy hair in the woods. Here comb is a verb meaning to search a place very carefully. Ask yourself: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Check a meaning by putting it into the sentence. «Bees store honey in a honeycomb.» A honeycomb is the wax cells bees make, not a tool for hair. When one meaning does not fit, try another.
fact: A comb is a flat strip with a row of thin teeth, used to tidy hair.
fact: To comb your hair means to make it neat with a comb.
fact: To comb a place means to search it very carefully.
fact: A comb can also be the red crest on a rooster's head.
fact: Head lice are tiny insects that live in hair; «anyone can get them, clean or dirty», and a fine-tooth comb helps to find them.
exercise: «She ran a comb through her wet hair.» What is a comb in this sentence? | *A flat strip with thin teeth, for tidying hair | A wax cell that bees make | A red crest on a rooster's head
exercise: «The police combed the woods for the missing child.» What does combed mean here? | Brushed gently | Tidied with a comb | Covered in wax | *Searched very carefully
exercise: Mia says, «The rooster's comb is bright red.» What is the comb? | *The red crest on the rooster's head | A flat strip with thin teeth | The wax cells bees make | A tool for brushing horses
test: «Bees store honey in a honeycomb.» What does honeycomb mean here? | A red crest on a bird | A flat strip with thin teeth | A search of the hive | *Wax cells that bees make
test: «The nurse used a fine-tooth comb to check for head lice.» What does this suggest about the check? | Loud and messy | Done in a hurry | *Very careful and thorough | Quick and careless
exam: Jayden says, «A comb is always for hair.» Is he right? | Yes, but only when it is wet | No, a comb is always a bird | *No, comb also means to search carefully, a rooster's crest or a honeycomb | Yes, comb only ever means a tool for hair
